capture: reflection-based annotation scanning
runtime: Java 17
commit: migration change set, a verified successor of base commit c8f6226105.
  No hash is recorded here, and none is invented: the revision that carries
  this file cannot be written inside the file it commits.  The relationship
  was measured rather than asserted - git merge-base --is-ancestor reports
  that the base commit is an ancestor of this working tree's head - so the
  descriptor names the revision precisely without fabricating a value for it.
  The counterpart region carries the literal base-commit hash instead,
  because a baseline describes a revision that already existed when it was
  written, and this side does not.  That asymmetry is the one legitimate
  difference in this field between the two capture directories.
source: application log during container startup
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no six-service reference stack and no servlet container on this
  host, therefore no container startup log from which an annotation-scanning
  region could be selected.  Measured on this host rather than assumed, and
  every value below was read back before this file was written: the hostname
  arkcase-ce.local does not resolve; anonymous probes of the application
  context and of the search service each returned 000, meaning no HTTP
  response was received at all rather than an error response; TOMCAT_HOME,
  CATALINA_HOME and CATALINA_LOG are all unset and no catalina.sh is on
  PATH, so there is no servlet container to start; and a search of the whole
  working tree found zero .war files, so there is no archive to deploy.  A
  further, independent blocker: the designated producer named above refuses
  to start unless an administrator credential is supplied through its
  environment, so no run of it was possible here even setting the absent
  stack aside, and supplying administrator material would have been neither
  available nor appropriate.  The migrated runtime is deliberately NOT listed
  among the unmet preconditions, because it is not one: Java 17 is installed
  on this host and answers 17.0.19, and the sibling env/toolchain.txt records
  that same banner for this side of the comparison.  What is missing is the
  deployment and its backing services, not the JVM.  The counterpart region
  recorded the mirror-image finding for the older runtime, which is present
  on this host too, so the two sides are absent for the same reason rather
  than for two different ones and the delta between them is the runtime
  label alone.
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is
  nevertheless stated in full, so that a later run reproduces the same
  transformation rather than inventing one, and so that a reader can see
  which differences between the two sides would have been suppressed and
  which would have been allowed to show.  When this region is captured the
  pipeline strips timestamps and dates, thread names and thread identifiers,
  session identifiers, generated request and instance identifiers, absolute
  filesystem paths in favour of a stable token, port assignments, and
  elapsed-time and duration figures.  It preserves the behaviour-bearing
  content, which in this region is unusually concentrated: the scanned
  package and URL names, the scanned-type counts, the discovered class and
  interface names, the names of the consuming components, and the full
  message text of every warning and error line.  Over-normalising this
  region would destroy the very evidence it exists to carry, because here the
  counts and the class names ARE the behaviour, and it is the dangerous
  failure rather than the visible one, because it makes two captures agree
  for a reason unrelated to behaviour.
  One tension inside this region is resolved deliberately rather than left to
  chance, and the exact rule is written down here so that a reviewer can
  check that both sides used the same one.  Classpath scanning output
  routinely carries absolute jar and directory paths, and those paths are
  volatile between two hosts.  THE RULE: the volatile absolute prefix - every
  path segment up to and including the local repository or deployment root -
  is replaced with a stable token, and the artefact filename and the package
  or class name that follow it are PRESERVED verbatim, including the
  artefact's version segment where the path carries one.  Which artefact was
  scanned and which package was found are behaviour; the directory it
  happened to sit in is not.  A blanket path rewrite that swallowed the
  artefact name would leave this region with nothing to compare, and a
  capture left full of absolute paths would not diff against its counterpart
  at all; the rule does both jobs rather than choosing one.  It is stated
  identically in the counterpart region.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured,
  every credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder
  <REDACTED>, which is the convention this documentation tree already uses
  and which the repository itself already applies.  The scan that finds them
  looks for password=, passwd, user=, secret and token=, and every matched
  value is expected to read <REDACTED> afterwards.  Scanning output is not a
  usual carrier of credentials, but two carriers reach this region in
  practice and both are treated identically: a datasource or
  directory-service credential echoed by a component whose initialisation
  interleaves with a scan, and keystore or trust-store passwords echoed by a
  container that prints its own launch arguments, which are recorded only in
  the redacted forms keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and
  trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>.  The documented administrator credential is
  never transcribed here in any form, not even as an illustration of what
  redaction removes.  A third carrier is handled by description instead of
  replacement, and is set out in the notes below.
