----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.
  Because the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no
  log line was invented to fill the gap.  Fabricating scan lines here would
  have asserted that scanning completed across the permission evaluator when
  that was never observed, which would destroy both the comparison this
  directory exists to support and the preserve-list evidence this region is
  supposed to carry.  An explicit, machine-readable skip with a reason is
  rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is strictly worse than an
  honest absence.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record
  an absence in both places.  The sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt
  reaches the same verdict for the eight application flows and records the
  comparator's overall INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result; this region is
  consistent with that record rather than an exception to it.  Nothing here
  should be read as clearing the deployment gate.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that
  the producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why
  the delimiters are empty.  Two independent facts corroborate that.  First,
  the producing script refuses to start without an administrator credential
  supplied through its environment, so no run was possible here even setting
  the absent stack aside.  Second, that script's startup-region routine is
  driven by an optional container log supplied through its environment, and
  when that log is unset or unreadable it writes an explicit unavailability
  record and returns without mining any region at all; exactly that file -
  startup/unavailable.log, byte-identical on both sides of the comparison -
  is the only other file in this directory.  The absence is therefore
  recorded twice, by two different mechanisms, and agrees with itself.
- Region-selection rule, identical to the rule the counterpart region states,
  so that the two sides selected the same window rather than merely claiming
  to.  From the first-startup window, select every line emitted by the
  reflection-based classpath scanning library and every line emitted by the
  seven consuming components named below while a scan is in progress: the
  packages or classpath URLs handed to each scanner, the scanner types
  configured, the counts and names of the types, fields and methods
  discovered, the successful completion of each scan, and every warning or
  error line the scanning library raises.  Select nothing else.  The window
  is the same window every sibling startup region is drawn from, so the
  regions partition that window instead of overlapping it.
- Selection boundary that is easy to get wrong in both directions.  Selecting
  only lines whose logger belongs to the scanning library would silently drop
  the consuming components' own before-and-after lines, which is where the
  discovered counts actually surface; and selecting the whole
  context-initialisation window would duplicate a sibling region and make the
  mirror diff report the same bytes twice.  The rule above is written to the
  components rather than to the logger for that reason.
- Why this region exists at all, stated precisely.  The reflection-based
  scanning library reads RAW CLASS BYTES to find annotations and sub-types.
  That makes it a textbook candidate blocker on a new class-file major
  version, and it is thoroughly load-bearing in this application, including
  underneath the permission evaluator.  It was nevertheless deliberately HELD
  at its existing version, on the strength of the migration's own assessment
  - an end-to-end scan of a class file at major version 61 succeeded on the
  installed release with its existing companions.  That assessment is cited
  here as the migration's finding, not as something this capture witnessed;
  what this capture contributes is the migrated half of the comparison the
  assessment is checked against.  Holding a version after assessment is as
  much a rule-compliant decision as changing one, and it needs evidence too.
  This region is that evidence on the migrated side.
- Governing rule on justified changes, the rule this region evidences, quoted
  from the requirements: "Every dependency version change must have a
  specific Java 17 or Node 20 compatibility reason... A change without a
  reason is out of scope."  Its CONTRAPOSITIVE is what applies here, and it
  is load-bearing rather than a debating point: bumping the scanning library
  or its class-parsing companion without a demonstrated incompatibility would
  ITSELF violate that rule, exactly as firmly as omitting a needed fix would.
  Both were therefore assessed and both were held.  A reviewer looking for
  the change this region justifies will not find one, and that absence is the
  finding rather than an oversight.
- Measured evidence that holding was the decision, re-derived at the base
  commit and again against this working tree so that the pair can be
  compared.  The scanning library's version property reads 0.9.9 at
  pom.xml:136 at the base commit, with its managed entry at
  pom.xml:1642-1644; the same property still reads 0.9.9 after the migration,
  at pom.xml:253 with its managed entry at pom.xml:1999-2003 in this tree.
  The line numbers moved because the migration rewrote the surrounding
  property block; the VALUE did not move, which is the whole point.  A held
  version is verifiable by comparison and does not have to be taken on trust.
  command: git show c8f6226105:pom.xml | grep -o '<reflections.version>[^<]*<'
  command: grep -o '<reflections.version>[^<]*<' pom.xml
  measured-version-at-base-commit: 0.9.9
  measured-version-in-this-tree: 0.9.9
- The class-parsing companion is not declared by this repository at all, at
  either revision, and that is worth recording because it changes where a
  reviewer should look.  The scanning library's own published POM declares
  the companion as a non-optional dependency, so it arrives transitively and
  that is the version any scan actually loads.  The ONLY mention of a
  same-named artefact anywhere in the 145 POMs, at the base commit and in
  this tree alike, is an EXCLUSION of the LEGACY groupId form from an
  unrelated module dependency, at
  acm-services/acm-service-calendar-integration-exchange/pom.xml:30-33.  That
  is a different groupId from the one the scanning library pulls, and the
  block is byte-identical at both revisions, so the migration did not touch
  it.  Anyone searching for a direct declaration of the companion will find
  only that exclusion, and would be wrong to read it as the version in use.
  command: grep -rn "javassist" --include=pom.xml .
  measured-matching-pom-files: 1
  measured-direct-declarations: 0
- Labelling convention for the rest of this block, stated once so that no
  line in it has to be guessed at.  There are exactly three kinds of
  statement below and they are not interchangeable.  A line carrying a
  "command:" and a "measured-" figure is an OBSERVATION OF THE SOURCE TREE,
  re-derived on this host by running that command against this working tree
  or the stated revision; it is fact, and it is reproducible.  A line
  prefixed "expected-evidence:" is an EXPECTATION, derived from the
  migration's own assessment or from reading the repository, and it was NOT
  witnessed on this host, because no run happened here.  Everything else is
  prose explaining one of those two.  Conflating the first with the second is
  exactly the error that makes a capture unfalsifiable, so the distinction is
  made explicit rather than left to the reader.
- expected-evidence: the reason NEITHER library moved.  The class-parsing
  companion was probed across a wide release range and every release tested
  parsed a class file at major version 61, because its class-file reader never
  enforced a maximum major version; and the scanner API is stable across the
  relevant release range.  With the parsing floor cleared and the API
  unchanged, there was no compatibility reason to advance either artefact, so
  neither was advanced.  The collections library the scanner depends on was
  assessed on the same basis and likewise held.
