- Why this evidence belongs to a STARTUP region rather than to a flow capture.
  The permission evaluator's scan runs from afterPropertiesSet(), so it is
  driven by the container's bean lifecycle and completes before any request is
  served.  A capture taken during an application flow would observe the
  CONSEQUENCES of that scan without ever observing the scan, which is why the
  scan is archived here and the permission-dependent flow assertions live in
  the flow captures.
- PRESERVE-list obligation, honoured explicitly.  Security and
  permission-evaluation outcomes are on the migration's PRESERVE list.  The
  permission evaluator and the reflection scanning that supports it are
  UNCHANGED: no signature, no scanned package, no scanner configuration and
  no library version differs between the two sides.  Nothing in this file
  implies, proposes or performs any change to the permission evaluator or to
  any permission-evaluation outcome, and this region is where a reviewer would
  see that the scan completed identically rather than being told that it did.
  Because the region is empty, that confirmation is OWED rather than
  delivered, and saying so plainly is the only honest reading of an
  unobserved preserve-list guarantee.
- NEGATIVE FINDING 1, re-derived here rather than carried forward: every
  setAccessible call site in this repository targets the application's OWN
  classes, never a JDK-internal member.  Verified as exactly four call sites
  repository-wide, all four in main source, all four on a
  java.lang.reflect.Field, and the same four at the base commit and in this
  tree.  There are ZERO setAccessible call sites in test source, so the
  repository-wide total and the main-source total are the same number.
  command: grep -rn "setAccessible" --include=*.java . | grep "/src/main/java/"
