  ZERO of the four names a JDK-internal type or a JDK-internal member.  Why
  that settles the question rather than merely looking reassuring: strong
  encapsulation bites only where code reflects ACROSS a module boundary into a
  package that module does not export.  Every one of the four reflects into an
  ArkCase domain or utility class that the application itself loaded, so no
  boundary is crossed, there is nothing for the platform to refuse, and not
  one of them needs a relaxed module boundary.  This is half of what earns the
  empty register of JDK-internal-access exceptions described further below.
- NEGATIVE FINDING 2, and it has to be stated precisely or a reviewer who runs
  the obvious command will conclude the finding is false.  The finding is that
  there is ZERO java.lang.SecurityManager usage in this repository.  That is
  measured, not inferred, and it holds at the base commit and in this tree
  alike.  Nothing installs, subclasses, or interacts with the platform's own
  security manager anywhere in the application.
  command: grep -rn "java.lang.SecurityManager" --include=*.java . | wc -l
