- The sharpest part of that breakdown, and the reason the finding is worth
  stating at all: AcmWebDAVSecurityManager.java:38 reads, in full, "public
  interface AcmWebDAVSecurityManager extends SecurityManager".  Read on its
  own that line looks exactly like the application extending the platform's
  security manager.  It is not.  The import at :32 resolves that simple name
  to the WebDAV library's own SecurityManager interface, and the platform's
  security manager is never named anywhere in the file or anywhere in the
  repository.  A reviewer who stops at :38 without reading :32 will reach the
  opposite conclusion from the correct one.
- Disclosed divergence from the migration plan's prose, on the same principle
  as the arithmetic disclosure above.  The plan describes all 29 occurrences
  as referring to the application's own AcmWebDAVSecurityManager.  The count
  of 29 is confirmed exactly, and the conclusion that matters is confirmed
  exactly at zero platform-security-manager references.  The characterisation
  is narrower than the evidence: the 29 comprise the application's own
  AcmWebDAVSecurityManager family AND the third-party WebDAV library's
  same-named interface family, as itemised above.  The verified figures and
  the commands that produced them are recorded here, and the plan's wording is
  noted rather than contradicted silently.  The verified breakdown is the
  stronger statement in any case, because "all 29 live in one module and none
  of them is the platform's" is a tighter bound than the plan's.
- What those two findings are FOR.  Together they are why the register of
  JDK-internal-access exceptions is delivered EMPTY as an EARNED result rather
  than as an assumption: production code reflects only into its own classes,
  and no platform security manager is in play, so production launch
  configuration has nothing to open.  That register is referred to here BY
  TITLE ONLY.  Its title is JDK Access Exceptions, and its filename is
  deliberately not written anywhere in this file, because that filename embeds
  a token this documentation tree excludes; naming the file would defeat the
  very audit the register exists to satisfy.
- The third finding behind that emptiness belongs to the register rather than
  to this window, and it is reported here as the register states it rather than
  in the narrower form the plan uses, because a register that under-reports its
  own scope is the failure it exists to prevent.  The register records FIVE
  places where strong encapsulation refused a pinned dependency on the target
  runtime - TWO in test libraries and THREE in production dependencies, the
  clearest production cases being rule compilation and the deserialisation of
  date-and-time values - plus one compile-time call site in the application's
  own source.  None is an exception in the sense the register counts, because
  in every case the DEPENDENCY WAS FIXED instead: each was resolved by
  upgrading or removing the library that failed, or by rewriting the call site,
  never by relaxing the platform's module boundaries.  That is precisely the
  remedy the governing transformation rule prescribes - fix the library, do not
  open the module - and it is why the register is empty rather than merely
  unpopulated.  The plan's own wording describes every such failure as being
  inside a test library; the register's measured account is broader, and the
  broader account is reported here rather than the tidier one.  Either way the
  conclusion this region depends on is unchanged and is the stronger of the
  two: nothing was opened, so production launch configuration declares no
  exception, and the scanning library in particular needed none.
- Substitution policy for module-access arguments, and this region is at
  elevated risk of tripping it precisely because reflective access is its
  subject - it would be easy to slip a literal argument spelling into prose
  while explaining why none is needed.  If a captured line ever carried a
  module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an otherwise-encapsulated
  JDK package to the unnamed module, that argument would be replaced in this
  region by that description in words, and the substitution would be recorded
  both in this notes block and in the redacted field above.  Verified in this
  tree: the audited production launch configuration declares no such argument,
  and the target runtime needs none, so on this side there was nothing to
  substitute and a faithful capture would be clean.  The fallback is written
  down so that a future run has a defined action rather than a judgement call.
  command: the launch-configuration block in this tree was extracted with sed
    and counted with grep -c against the three module-access argument
    spellings.  The pattern itself is deliberately not transcribed on this
    line, because writing it would introduce into this file the very token the
    recursive audit of this documentation tree requires to be absent, and a
    self-defeating command line is worse than a described one.
  measured-module-access-arguments-in-launch-configuration: 0
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers reflection-based annotation scanning
  ONLY.  Container and application startup belongs to catalina.out.log; Spring
  context initialisation, aspects and the directory-service evidence to
  spring-context.log; persistence initialisation to jpa-init.log;
  workflow-engine initialisation to workflow-engine-init.log; the startup asset
  build to frontend-build.log; the consolidated error-severity scan to
  error-scan.txt; and the readiness poll to readiness-poll.log.  Outside this
  directory, toolchain provenance belongs to ../env/toolchain.txt, artifact
