- expected-evidence: the shape of a completed scan.  Each of the seven scanner
  constructions itemised above - across six constructing consumers, with the
  camel context manager contributing two - is expected to emit the configured
  package or classpath URL set it was handed, and its consuming component is
  expected to record the count and the names of the types, fields or methods
  the scan returned.  Counts and names are PRESERVED by the normalisation
  policy, not stripped, because they are the behaviour: an identical count of
  identically named discovered types on both sides is what "scanning completed
  identically" means, and a region that had normalised them away could not
  demonstrate it.
- expected-evidence: the permission evaluator's scan is expected to complete
  during context initialisation and before the application answers a request,
  because it runs from afterPropertiesSet().  A region in which the application
  answers before that scan appears is presumptively truncated or mis-selected.
- ADDITIONAL CAPTURE PRECONDITION, stated here because it applies whether the
  region is captured or skipped, and because getting it wrong in either
  direction is a reporting failure rather than a technical one.  The consuming
  components and the scanning library may log at a level that is not enabled by
  default in a deployed configuration, so a run made at a coarse level can
  yield a THIN region - a handful of lines, or none - even when every scan
  actually completed normally.  The ambiguity is resolved explicitly rather
  than left to the reader.  A thin region MUST be reported as thin, with the
  effective log level recorded beside it, and it must NOT be presented as a
  region that proves nothing happened: a region with little in it and a region
  that demonstrates an absence are different findings, and only the second is
  evidence of anything.  Conversely a thin region must not be presented as a
  successful comparison either, because a comparison over two nearly empty
  regions agrees for a reason unrelated to behaviour.  The defined action is to
  record the level, record the line count, and say which of the two readings
  the evidence supports; if it supports neither, say that.  For THIS file the
  distinction does not arise, because the region is not thin but EMPTY, and it
  is empty for a reason that has nothing to do with log level: no container ran
  at all, as the skip-reason above records with the measurement behind each
  unmet precondition.  No claim is made here about what any log level would
  have shown.
- expected-evidence: warning lines from a class-byte-reading scanner are
  ordinary in a healthy first startup of an application this size, typically
  where a scanned classpath entry cannot be opened or a referenced type cannot
  be resolved.  They would be captured as observed, with their message text
  preserved in full, and they are not treated as migration regressions merely
  by existing.  The comparison that matters is with the counterpart region.  The
  consolidated error-severity scan is a sibling file drawn from this same
  window; this region is one of the windows that scan reads from, which is why
  the two must come from the same capture and must not be reconciled after the
  fact.
- expected-evidence: what a genuine regression would look like here, recorded so
  that a future capture is read against a criterion rather than an impression.
  A failure to read a class file at the new major version would surface as a
  class-format or unsupported-version error raised from the scanning library or
  its class-parsing companion, or as a discovered-type count that dropped below
  the counterpart count for the same scanned packages.  Either is a regression,
  and either would also be a direct falsification of the decision to hold the
  library rather than advance it.  A difference confined to a stripped
  timestamp, a thread name or an absolute path is not a regression, and the
  normalisation policy above exists so that such differences never reach the
  comparison in the first place.
- expected-evidence: a regression in this region would additionally be a
  preserve-list finding rather than merely a dependency finding, because the
  permission evaluator's authorisation decisions are downstream of the scan it
  performs at :505.  That is the sharpest reason this empty region is a debt
  rather than a neutral outcome, and it is stated plainly instead of being left
  implicit: the preserve-list guarantee on security and permission-evaluation
  outcomes is unverified at runtime on this host.  What IS verified, and stated
  above with the commands behind it, is that nothing in the change set altered
  the evaluator, its scanned packages, its scanner configuration or the library
  version it depends on.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is baseline/startup/reflection-scan.log:
  the same filename, the same field set in the same order, and the same
  region-selection and normalisation rules stated above, so that a recursive
  diff between the two capture directories is mechanically meaningful.  No
  field was added on this side that the counterpart lacks, and none was
  dropped.  Only two field values differ legitimately - the runtime label,
  which reads Java 17 here, and the commit descriptor, which names this change
  set rather than the base commit.  A field added on one side only, or a region
  selected differently on one side only, would register as a difference that
  carries no behavioural signal at all.  The figures in this file that were
  re-derived from the repository - the seven consumers, the six modules, the
  seven scanner constructions, the four setAccessible sites, the 29 bare-name
  occurrences, the zero platform-security-manager references and the held
  library version - are properties of the source tree rather than of a run, so
  both sides report the same values; where a published figure differs from the
  counterpart's prose it is disclosed above as a divergence rather than left for
  a reviewer to trip over.
